"@type": "Question",
"name": "When do the new permanent residency rules start?",
"acceptedAnswer": {
"@type": "Answer",
"text": " Japan's Immigration Services Agency revised its Guidelines for Permission for Permanent Residence on October 1, 2026. Most of the new rules apply to applications filed on or after April 1, 2027.

"
}
}
, {
"@type": "Question",
"name": "Do the new rules apply if I file before April 2027?",
"acceptedAnswer": {
"@type": "Answer",
"text": " Mostly no, but there is one big exception. The revised income and public-burden provisions also apply to applications filed on or after April 1, 2026 that were still pending on October 1, 2026, and to anything filed after that through March 31, 2027. Do not assume an application filed before April 2027 will be judged entirely under the old rules.

"
}
}
, {
"@type": "Question",
"name": "What Japanese level do I need to apply for Permanent Residence in Japan?",
"acceptedAnswer": {
"@type": "Answer",
"text": " The guideline expects roughly B1 on the Framework of Reference for Japanese-Language Education, which is modeled on the CEFR. B1 sits between a high JLPT N3 and a low JLPT N2.

"
}
}
, {
"@type": "Question",
"name": "Who is exempt from the B1 expectation?",
"acceptedAnswer": {
"@type": "Answer",
"text": " Highly skilled foreign professionals and their family members, applicants who received at least six years of elementary or secondary education under Japan's School Education Act, and children of permanent residents who meet the guideline's conditions.

"
}
}
, {
"@type": "Question",
"name": "Do late tax or pension payments matter when I am applying for Permanent Residence in Japan?",
"acceptedAnswer": {
"@type": "Answer",
"text": " Yes. Immigration authorities look closely at late payments of taxes, pension contributions, and health insurance. They are a common reason for denial, because meeting public obligations is a key part of the screening.
